            <ArticleTitle>Primary Corneoscleral Cyst in a Pediatric Patient.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>425-428</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1159/000477378</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Primary corneoscleral cyst is a rare disease occurring in the pediatric age group. We report a case of corneoscleral cyst with visual diminution.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We conducted a case report.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">A 7-year-old girl presented with corneal opacity in the left eye. Examination revealed a corneoscleral cyst. The corneal part of the cyst involved visual axis. Surgical excision with a scleral graft was performed, leading to an improvement in visual acuity. No recurrences were observed until the last follow-up at 18 months.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Corneoscleral cyst should be considered in the differential diagnosis of cystic ocular surface disorders in the pediatric age group.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
